\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Canonical commutation and Weyl operators}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{On compactly supported smooth functions, the directional
derivative #{D_z} along a real displacement #{z} and multiplication
by #{\langle r,q\rangle} satisfy
##{[D_z,\Phi(r)]=\langle r,z\rangle\bbI}
by the product rule. Physics writes #{\Pi(z)=-\ui D_z}, and then
##{[\Phi(r),\Pi(z)]=\ui\langle r,z\rangle\bbI.}
The scalar #{\ui} makes #{\Pi(z)} self-adjoint. One field
coordinate is a line: it has no unit bivector of a plane and no
central pseudoscalar of square #{-1}. The Weyl operators use the
same scalar as the character of that translation.}
